   <front>
     <title>Transmission of Multiple Control Messages</title>
     <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="23" stream="IETF"/>
+    <author fullname="G. Gregg" initials="G." surname="Gregg">
+      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">UCSB</organization>
+      <address/>
+    </author>
     <date year="1969" month="October"/>
     <abstract pn="section-abstract">
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">The network program at a site must be prepared to send or receive more
+than one control message in a single control communication.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-2">During the time that the control link is blocked awaiting RFNM, it is
+possible that a number of control messages to a particular remote host
+will accumulate.  When the link is unblocked, these should all be sent
+in a single communication for greatest efficiency.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-3">For receiving, it will be necessary to have a loop which returns to
+decode and take action on successive control messages until the control
+communication is exhausted.</t>
     </abstract>
